<header><p class="st-kicker">crusader state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Edessa</h1><p class="st-meta">1098 CE – 1150 CE · capital Edessa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Crusader state, founded on the upper Euphrates; its fall to Zengi in 1144 launched the Second Crusade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1098 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin of Boulogne</a> left the main army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> and went east at the invitation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoros-of-edessa">Thoros</a>, the Armenian ruler of Edessa, who adopted him as son and heir. Weeks later Thoros was killed in a riot and Baldwin took the city, founding the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> state in the East.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county stretched across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turbessel">Turbessel</a> west of the river and Edessa east of it. A small Frankish elite ruled a population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac-christians">Syriac Christians</a> and relied on intermarriage with Armenian nobility. Its counts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joscelin-i-count-of-edessa">Joscelin I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joscelin-ii-of-edessa">Joscelin II</a>, were exposed on every side; the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> in 1104 halted their advance towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a> took Edessa on 24 December 1144 while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joscelin-ii-of-edessa">Joscelin II</a> was absent, provoking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>. Joscelin was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> in 1150, and his wife Beatrice sold the remaining fortresses to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>, which soon lost them.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-edessa.jpg" alt="County of Edessa" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dragovit · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_the_County_of_Edessa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1098 CE – 1150 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>crusader state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Edessa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Baldwin of Boulogne, Baldwin II, Joscelin II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin Christianity, Armenian and Syriac Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Armenian, Syriac</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Edessa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1124&amp;focus=county-of-edessa" aria-label="County of Edessa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5651 839 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Edessa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5971 1095L5908 1277M5971 1095L5936 1127M5971 1095L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5908 1277h0M5936 1127h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5971 1095h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5971 1095h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Edessa. <a href="/atlas?year=1124&amp;focus=county-of-edessa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
